<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Maximilian von Montgelas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1759">1759</a>–<a href="/years/1838">1838</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a> · Also known as Count Montgelas</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Maximilian von Montgelas was a Bavarian statesman who, as chief minister of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maximilian-i-joseph-of-bavaria">Maximilian IV Joseph</a>, later King Maximilian I, from 1799 to 1817, created the modern Bavarian state. Allied with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a>, he greatly enlarged <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>'s territory and saw it become a kingdom in 1806, then centralised the administration, secularised monasteries, curbed noble privileges and introduced religious toleration. His reforms prepared the way for the constitution of 1818.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/maximilian-von-montgelas.jpg" alt="Maximilian von Montgelas" width="320" height="401" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Joseph Hauber · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Maximilian_von_Montgelas_(1806).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Montgelas came of a noble family of the Duchy of Savoy. His father, Janus Sigmund Garnerin, Baron Montgelas, served Maximilian III, Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and married Countess Ursula von Trauner; Maximilian was their eldest son, born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>. He was educated at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nancy">Nancy</a>, Strasbourg and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ingolstadt">Ingolstadt</a> and all his life wrote French more easily than German, though he wished to be counted a Bavarian. He entered the censorship of books in 1779, but the Elector Charles Theodore turned against him on learning that he belonged to the Illuminati, the anti-clerical <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/secret-society">secret society</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>He moved to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zweibrucken">Zweibrücken</a> and became private secretary to Maximilian Joseph, the Duke's brother, who made him a minister on succeeding to the duchy; Montgelas attended the Second Congress of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rastatt">Rastatt</a> in 1798. As early as 1796, while his master was a landless exile in Ansbach, he had drawn up a plan for modernising <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, the Ansbacher mémoire, rediscovered in the 1960s and published in 1970 by his biographer Eberhard Weis. He concluded the Treaty of Bogenhausen with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> in August 1805, holding that Bavaria could stand against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/austria">Austria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> only with French help. German opponents of French supremacy saw him as the type of the unpatriotic politician.</p><p>At home he taxed the nobility and clergy, built a full cadastral survey, issued the constitution of 1808, which ended the remains of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serfdom">serfdom</a>, and abolished torture with a new penal code in 1812. He introduced compulsory schooling, military service and vaccination, replaced the old chambers with modern ministries, removed internal tolls and, through the Dienstpragmatik, opened the civil service to the educated regardless of birth or faith. Protestants gained civil rights and the Jewish communities a secure, though still discriminatory, legal status. He kept the parliament promised in 1808 from ever meeting.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>The decision to turn against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> in 1814 was taken under the influence of the king's son and Marshal Wrede rather than Montgelas, and his enemies persuaded the king to dismiss him in 1817. He married Countess von Arco in 1803, had eight children and was made a count in 1809. He sat in the Bavarian House of Lords until his death in 1838. In 2005 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a> raised a monument to him at the Promenadenplatz.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Maximilian von Montgelas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>His chief minister, <b>Maximilian von Montgelas</b>, had steered Bavaria into alliance with Napoleon, who offered it protection against Austria and territorial gains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-bavaria">Kingdom of Bavaria · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/munich">Munich</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1810" aria-label="Munich on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4819 479 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Maximilian von Montgelas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5269 749h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.14,11.58&amp;year=1810">What was here in 1810? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
